Given the parametric equation of curve $C_1$ as $$\begin{cases} x=2-t \\ y= \sqrt {3}t \end{cases}$$ (where $t$ is the parameter), when $t=-1$, it corresponds to a point A on curve $C_1$ and the symmetric point of A with respect to the origin is B. Establishing a coordinate system with the origin O as the pole and the x-axis as the positive semi-axis, the polar equation of curve $C_2$ is $\rho= \frac {6}{ \sqrt {9-3\sin^{2}\theta}}$.  
